# ── Settings: security ────────────────────────────────────────

[settings.security]
Security

[settings.security.blurb]
Guests are streamed nothing until you allow them, every time they connect.

[settings.security.approval_timeout]
Approval timeout

[settings.security.approval_timeout_hint]
How long a guest waits for an answer before being refused. Timing out denies —
an unattended machine should not share its screen.

[settings.security.admin_code]
Admin code

[settings.security.admin_autoreset]
Reset automatically

[settings.security.admin_autoreset_hint]
Admin codes grant full control immediately — resetting often is safer

[settings.security.guest_code]
Guest code

[settings.security.guest_autoreset]
Reset when the last guest leaves

[settings.security.give_way]
Interrupt guest input

[settings.security.give_way_hint]
When you use this machine's own mouse or keyboard, a guest's input is ignored
for this long, then resumes on its own.

Nothing a guest is holding can be left stuck down. Zero turns this off.

[settings.security.trusted_devices]
Trusted devices

[settings.security.trusted_none]
None yet

[settings.security.trusted_count.one]
1 device

[settings.security.trusted_count.other]
{n} devices

[settings.security.forget_all]
Forget all

[settings.security.forget_all_hint]
Devices that have connected with the admin passcode and been allowed. Forgetting
them means the next connection from each one has to be approved again. Resetting
the admin passcode does this too, along with revoking the code itself.

[settings.security.offer_by_default]
Offer by default

[settings.security.offer_hint]
Which boxes start ticked in the approval dialog. You still have to allow each
connection. Mouse also covers touch.

[settings.security.sensitivity_hint]
The starting value for new sessions. It only applies while the host has captured
its pointer — a game being looked around in — and can be changed during a
session by right-clicking the pointer button on its toolbar.

[settings.security.pre_ticked_warning]
⚠ Input will be pre-ticked — check the dialog before allowing a guest.


# ── Settings: network ─────────────────────────────────────────

[settings.network]
Network

[settings.network.open_links]
Open tain:// links with this copy of Tain

[settings.network.open_links_hint]
A link copied from the header opens Tain and connects in one click. This is
registered for your Windows account only. Turn it off to leave links to
another copy of Tain.

[settings.network.relays]
Your own relays

[settings.network.relays_blurb]
Two machines meet on a relay before they connect directly. Tain uses n0's
public relays; you can add iroh relay servers of your own, beside them or
instead of them.

[settings.network.relays_hint]
relay.example.org — one per line

[settings.network.relay_invalid]
⚠ Not a relay address, so not used: {entry}

[settings.network.relays_only]
Use only these relays

[settings.network.relays_only_hint]
Never use n0's relays. This machine can then be reached only through yours,
so a guest has to be able to reach your relay from wherever they are.

[settings.network.relays_none]
Only n0's relays are used.

[settings.network.relays_beside_note]
Tain uses whichever relay answers fastest, and yours if n0's can't be reached.
Codes and links carry the relay this machine is on, so guests need no setup.

[settings.network.relays_only_note]
Only your relays are used. Codes and links carry the one this machine is on,
so guests need no setup.

[settings.network.relays_restart]
⚠ Restart Tain to use these relays.


