# ── Settings: virtual display ─────────────────────────────────

[settings.vdisplay]
Virtual display

[settings.vdisplay.blurb]
A display that exists only in software. Lets a machine with no monitor be
streamed at all, gives a guest a screen at its own resolution instead of a
scaled copy of this one, and turns a spare tablet or laptop into a second
screen.

[settings.vdisplay.install]
Install driver

[settings.vdisplay.install_hint]
Windows will ask twice — once for administrator rights, and once about trusting
the publisher. The second prompt is Windows' own and cannot be skipped, which is
rather the point of it. The name it shows is the driver's author, not Tain: the
package is installed exactly as they signed it, because changing a byte of it
would void the signature.

[settings.vdisplay.remove_driver]
Remove driver

[settings.vdisplay.remove_driver_hint]
Removes the driver and any display it was presenting. Everything else keeps
working. Windows will ask for permission.

[settings.vdisplay.not_bundled]
This build was made without a driver package to install.

[settings.vdisplay.count.zero]
No virtual display.

[settings.vdisplay.count.one]
One virtual display.

[settings.vdisplay.count.other]
{n} virtual displays.

[settings.vdisplay.follow_guest]
Match the resolution of whoever connects

[settings.vdisplay.follow_guest_hint]
A virtual display takes the size of the guest watching it, so their window is
drawn 1:1 instead of scaled. Each display follows the first guest to watch it,
so a second guest gets a second display at their own size.

[settings.vdisplay.size]
Size

[settings.vdisplay.same_as_screen]
Same as this screen ({w}x{h})

[settings.vdisplay.add]
Add a display

[settings.vdisplay.add_hint]
Creates a virtual monitor and puts it on the desktop, to the right of everything
else. The display driver restarts to do it, so this takes a moment.

[settings.vdisplay.remove]
Remove

[settings.vdisplay.remove_hint]
Takes the most recently added virtual display away again.

[driver.ready]
Installed. A virtual display can be created.

[driver.idle]
Installed, but no virtual monitor is present yet.

[driver.not_installed]
Not installed. Virtual displays need it.

[driver.not_bundled]
This copy of Tain ships no driver to install.


# ── Settings: unattended access ───────────────────────────────

[settings.unattended]
Unattended access

[settings.unattended.blurb]
Reaching a UAC prompt, a lock screen, or a machine with nobody logged in needs a
Windows service. Everything else works without it.

[settings.unattended.install]
Install service

[settings.unattended.reinstall]
Reinstall service

[settings.unattended.install_hint]
Windows will ask for permission — registering a service needs administrator
rights, and Tain does not run with them.

[settings.unattended.uninstall]
Uninstall service

[settings.unattended.uninstall_hint]
Removes the service and everything it allowed. Tain keeps working for ordinary
sessions. Windows will ask for permission.

[settings.unattended.answer_signed_out]
Answer when nobody is signed in

[settings.unattended.answer_hint]
Hands this machine's identity to the service, so the same connection code
reaches it at the sign-in screen. Off, the service still covers consent prompts
and the lock screen while somebody is signed in.

[settings.unattended.needs_service]
Needs the service installed.

[settings.unattended.trust_note]
The service runs as LocalSystem so it can reach screens an ordinary program
cannot. That is a real amount of trust to place in this machine and in whoever
holds a code for it — install it only where you want unattended access.

[service.not_installed]
Not installed. Admin access needs it.

[service.running]
Installed and running.

[service.stopped]
Installed but not running — unattended access is unavailable.

[service.mismatched]
Installed, but registered against a different copy of Tain.

[service.unknown]
The service database could not be read.


