# ── What is holding the picture back ──────────────────────────

[bottleneck.slow_capture]
Slow capture

[bottleneck.slow_capture_advice]
The host cannot grab frames fast enough — usually another program saturating its
GPU. Stream settings will not help.

[bottleneck.slow_encode]
Slow encoding

[bottleneck.slow_encode_advice]
Encoding a frame takes longer than the frame budget. Enable the hardware encoder
on the host, or stream fewer pixels.

[bottleneck.slow_network]
Slow network

[bottleneck.slow_network_advice]
The link cannot carry the frames being produced. Lowering the bitrate or
resolution is what helps here.

[bottleneck.slow_decode]
Slow decoding

[bottleneck.slow_decode_advice]
This machine cannot decode frames as fast as they arrive. A lower resolution
costs it less work.

[bottleneck.slow_render]
Slow display

[bottleneck.slow_render_advice]
Frames decode in time but the window cannot draw them. Usually another program
competing for this machine's GPU.

[bottleneck.high_latency]
High latency

[bottleneck.high_latency_advice]
The round trip to the host is long — distance, or a relayed connection rather
than a direct one. Quality settings cannot shorten it.


# ── How the host was found ────────────────────────────────────

[found.lan]
LAN

[found.lan_detail]
Found on this local network by multicast — no relay, no DNS and no internet were
involved in locating this host.

[found.dns]
DNS

[found.dns_detail]
Found through the public lookup service, either as a DNS record or the same data
fetched over HTTPS.

[found.dht]
DHT

[found.dht_detail]
Found through the mainline DHT — the distributed table BitTorrent uses, which
has no operator and keeps working where DNS is blocked.

[found.code]
code

[found.code_detail]
Reached through the relay carried in the connection code. No address lookup
contributed anything, so the relay is the only route there was — which is why a
direct path has to be punched from it, and why there is nothing else to fall
back on if that fails.


# ── The steps of a connection attempt ─────────────────────────

[step.starting]
Starting up…

[step.reaching_relay]
Looking up where the host is…

[step.via_relay]
Reaching the host through its relay…

[step.direct]
Reaching the host directly…

[step.punching]
Opening a direct path…

[step.authenticating]
Sending the passcode…

[stalled.starting]
this machine could not open a connection of its own

[stalled.reaching_relay]
no relay could be reached from here, and the code carries no address to try
directly — ask for a fresh code, or connect once on a network that works so an
address can be remembered

[stalled.via_relay]
the host's relay could not be reached from here. The host may be fine and the
relay blocked

[stalled.direct]
the host did not answer on the address it last used. It may be switched off,
asleep, or on another network

[stalled.punching]
the host answered, but only through a relay — no direct path could be opened,
and a relay cannot carry video. A VPN or a strict NAT on either machine is the
usual reason

[stalled.authenticating]
the host answered but did not accept the passcode

[connect.unreachable]
Could not reach that host — {reason}


# ── The route the session took ────────────────────────────────

[path.direct]
Connected peer to peer.

[path.relayed]
Relayed through {host} because a direct connection could not be established.
Relays are shared and rate-limited, so video over one stutters no matter what
quality is chosen. A VPN on either machine is a common cause.

[path.relay_only]
Connected to the host through the relay {via}, but no direct path opened within
{wait}, so the session was refused rather than run over the relay — relays are
shared and rate limited, and video over one is a slideshow.

A VPN or tunnel on either machine is the usual cause, because it hides the
address the other side needs to punch to. A strict or symmetric NAT on either
side does the same. If both machines are on the same network, they should be
reachable directly: check that neither is on a guest or isolated Wi-Fi network,
and that the host's firewall is not blocking incoming UDP.

Connecting once from a network where a direct path does open teaches this
machine the host's address, and that address is tried first next time.


# ── Why a session ended ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# The host sends a short code, not a sentence, so each end renders the reason
# in its own language. `end.unknown` catches a code from a newer version.

[end.invalid_passcode]
that code is no longer valid — the host has generated a new one

[end.not_allowed]
the host did not allow this connection

[end.not_approved]
that code was accepted, but this device has not been approved on that machine
and there is nobody signed in there to approve it now. Connect once from a Tain
window on the host to approve this device — after that it can connect with
nobody at the machine.

[end.capture_failed]
the host could not capture its screen: {detail}

[end.capture_wrong_gpu_neither_way]
{screen} could not be captured either way the host knows. It is drawn by one of
two graphics chips, which rules out Desktop Duplication, and the compositor
refused it as well — so this is not simply the usual two-chip laptop. Check on
the host that the screen is still attached and that nothing else has it open,
and look at the host's log for what each attempt said.

[end.disconnected_by_host]
disconnected by the host

[end.host_closed]
the host closed Tain

[end.guest_closed]
the guest closed the session

[end.unknown]
the host ended the session ({code})


